@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.517 = private unnamed_addr constant [847 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for the unnecessary nesting of multiple consecutive context\0Amanagers.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python 3, a single `with` block can include multiple context\0Amanagers.\0A\0ACombining multiple context managers into a single `with` statement\0Awill minimize the indentation depth of the code, making it more\0Areadable.\0A\0AThe following context managers are exempt when used as standalone\0Astatements:\0A\0A - `anyio`.{`CancelScope`, `fail_after`, `move_on_after`}\0A - `asyncio`.{`timeout`, `timeout_at`}\0A - `trio`.{`fail_after`, `fail_at`, `move_on_after`, `move_on_at`}\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Awith A() as a:\0A    with B() as b:\0A        pass\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Awith A() as a, B() as b:\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: The `with` statement](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-with-statement)\0A", align 1
