@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.521 = private unnamed_addr constant [1250 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for subclasses of `collections.namedtuple` or `typing.NamedTuple`\0Athat lack a `__slots__` definition.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIn Python, the `__slots__` attribute allows you to explicitly define the\0Aattributes (instance variables) that a class can have. By default, Python\0Auses a dictionary to store an object's attributes, which incurs some memory\0Aoverhead. However, when `__slots__` is defined, Python uses a more compact\0Ainternal structure to store the object's attributes, resulting in memory\0Asavings.\0A\0ASubclasses of `namedtuple` inherit all the attributes and methods of the\0Abuilt-in `namedtuple` class. Since tuples are typically immutable, they\0Adon't require additional attributes beyond what the `namedtuple` class\0Aprovides. Defining `__slots__` for subclasses of `namedtuple` prevents the\0Acreation of a dictionary for each instance, reducing memory consumption.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom collections import namedtuple\0A\0A\0Aclass Foo(namedtuple(\22foo\22, [\22str\22, \22int\22])):\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom collections import namedtuple\0A\0A\0Aclass Foo(namedtuple(\22foo\22, [\22str\22, \22int\22])):\0A    __slots__ = ()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `__slots__`](https://docs.python.org/3/reference/datamodel.html#slots)\0A", align 1
