@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.620 = private unnamed_addr constant [18 x i8] c"too-many-arguments", align 1
@anon.b08dd5f4f09c4be6245191ddd275a566.621 = private unnamed_addr constant [951 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for function definitions that include too many arguments.\0A\0ABy default, this rule allows up to five arguments, as configured by the\0A[`lint.pylint.max-args`] option.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AFunctions with many arguments are harder to understand, maintain, and call.\0AConsider refactoring functions with many arguments into smaller functions\0Awith fewer arguments, or using objects to group related arguments.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef calculate_position(x_pos, y_pos, z_pos, x_vel, y_vel, z_vel, time):\0A    new_x = x_pos + x_vel * time\0A    new_y = y_pos + y_vel * time\0A    new_z = z_pos + z_vel * time\0A    return new_x, new_y, new_z\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom typing import NamedTuple\0A\0A\0Aclass Vector(NamedTuple):\0A    x: float\0A    y: float\0A    z: float\0A\0A\0Adef calculate_position(pos: Vector, vel: Vector, time: float) -> Vector:\0A    return Vector(*(p + v * time for p, v in zip(pos, vel)))\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.pylint.max-args`\0A", align 1
